```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("tools/guides/setup.R")
```

You run your own `pi-agent-core` `AgentHarness` host and want its lanes to
receive mail without ever losing or doubling a message. An interactive Pi
session acknowledges a message once Pi's public API accepts it; a harness host
can do better and commit the message to a lane of a durable session before it
acknowledges. `attachCoordinationLane` does that for one mailbox and one lane.

Three rules make it safe to crash anywhere:

- **Commit, then acknowledge.** The acknowledgement names the lane entry as its
  `delivery_ref`.
- **Reconcile redeliveries.** A redelivered message is matched to its existing
  lane entry by message ID, so a lost acknowledgement never queues a second
  copy.
- **Never drive.** The adapter queues with `steer` while a run is in progress
  and with `nextRun` otherwise. Starting runs, operation recovery and the
  model stay with the host.

## A host in forty lines

The example uses a faux model provider, so it needs no credentials. The
session, the lane, the endpoint and the recovery path are real:

```{bash}
sed -n '/^let opened/,/^await coordination.ready/p' tools/examples/harness-lane.ts
```

It sends two messages to the lane's mailbox. It drops the acknowledgement of
the second to stand in for a crash just after the lane commit, then reopens
the session from disk:

```{bash}
node tools/examples/harness-lane.ts
```

The redelivered message was recognised and acknowledged again with the same
lane entry. Both entries survive the restart, in order. The full script is
[`tools/examples/harness-lane.ts`](https://github.com/sounkou-bioinfo/pi-ducknng/blob/main/tools/examples/harness-lane.ts),
and [`test/coordination-harness.test.js`](https://github.com/sounkou-bioinfo/pi-ducknng/blob/main/test/coordination-harness.test.js)
checks the same behaviour.

## Choosing a durable session

A lane commit is only as durable as the session behind it. `JsonlSessionRepo`
writes to disk; an in-memory repository gives the same ordering but loses
everything with the process. The registration declares
`harness_lane_commit` either way, so choose the repository that makes that
claim true.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat("> GUIDE_HARNESS_VERIFIED: lane commit before acknowledgement, reconciliation, and recovery from disk\n")
```
